  "usermeta": {
    "title": "How Did Wave Conditions Change at Bilinga in 2025?",
    "analysis": "Bilinga's wave record shows two different forms of variation during 2025. May had the highest monthly median significant wave height (1.20 m), over twice July's 0.58 m, indicating notably stronger typical conditions in May. March, however, produced the year's exceptional peak of 4.87 m while its monthly median was 1.15 m. The contrast is striking: March experienced an intense upper extreme, whereas May led in typical wave height. March also contained 452 of the year's 521 readings at or above 2 m (87%), during the period when Tropical Cyclone Alfred generated substantial swell along southeast Queensland. August brought another noticeable maximum of 2.93 m alongside a relatively high median of 1.14 m. These differences show that the height of the largest observed waves and the conditions experienced most often tell complementary stories. Chart 6 now tests which monthly patterns repeat across multiple years.",
    "method": "Queensland Government Bilinga wave buoy: 30-minute 2025 AEST observations of significant wave height (Hs, metres). 515 sentinel readings of -99.9 excluded; 17,005 valid. Teal line = median of valid observations each month. Shaded band = observed minimum to maximum (not 25th–75th percentiles). Isolated extremes can strongly influence the monthly range.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "label": "Queensland Government — Bilinga 2025 Verified Wave Data",
        "url": "https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset/coastal-data-system-waves-bilinga/resource/f4a7339d-1e9a-464b-a780-f4e98c6cec8c"
      },
      {
        "label": "Bureau of Meteorology — Tropical Cyclone Alfred (2025)",
        "url": "https://www.bom.gov.au/cyclone/history/Alfred2025.shtml"
      }
    ]
  },
